# KNOWN GAP — `flat_map` that CHANGES the element type mints its accumulator
# from the INPUT element type, printing a raw pointer at rc 0.
#
#     Vector[int] nums = [3, 4]
#     auto v = nums.flat_map(tags)      # tags: (int) -> Vector[String]
#     print(v.get(0).unwrap())          # prints e.g. 187651182920432, not "tag"
#
# ⚠ TWO FALSE INVARIANT-ASSERTING COMMENTS sit on the defect and must go
# (Core #14 — an invariant comment needs an enforcing guard or it is deleted):
# `self_host_lowerer/lower_expr.gg:6775` and `:6791` both assert
#
#     "flat_map preserves the element type -- the closure returns a Vector of
#      the same element"
#
# which is FALSE against the typechecker's own signature at
# `src/semantic/typecheck.rs:8212`: `("Vector", "flat_map") => (T) -> Vector[U]`,
# whose comment even spells out the cross-type case. A reader who trusts the
# self-host comment will "clean up" the fix straight back into this bug.
#
# ⚠ DISCRIMINATORS, measured:
#   * Rust lane: `auto` destination is WRONG; a TYPED destination
#     (`Vector[String] v = ...`) with a named callee is CORRECT (prints "tag").
#   * Self-host lane: reported wrong on BOTH destinations, i.e. a lane
#     divergence with a direction -- Rust is ahead here, so fix the self-host.
#     (Rust-lane cells above are orchestrator-verified; the self-host column is
#     the R44 Track-K pass-8 measurement across five drivers.)
#
# ⚠ DISTINCT from the filed `map` sibling: that entry is scoped to `map` in its
# own text, and this is a different branch with its own mint site and its own
# pair of false comments. Same CLASS (accumulator minted from the input element
# type), different producer -- Core #4.
#
# INTENDED (asserted below): prints 2 then tag.
# Measured 2026-08-24 by the R44 Track-K brief-review pass 8, Rust lane
# orchestrator-verified.

String mk(String a, String b):
    return a + b

Vector[String] tags(int n):
    Vector[String] out = []
    out.push(mk("t", "ag"))
    return out

void main():
    Vector[int] nums = [3, 4]
    auto v = nums.flat_map(tags)
    print(v.len())
    print(v.get(0).unwrap())
